IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate
IMPORT `sg-core`
IMPORT `sg-periods`

-- Companies Act 1967 (Singapore), 2020 Revised Edition: s 161 -- approval of the company required for the issue of shares by directors.
-- Layer 1 (the law, objectively). Part sgb2 of the whole-Act encoding. Every top-level name begins with the label of its provision.
--
-- HOW IT READS. "Despite anything in a company's constitution" (s 161(1)) means no constitution can lift the bar: no input asks what
-- the constitution says. The approval is a fact a witness can state (the company in general meeting approved, on a date, for an exercise);
-- its life is worked out by s 161(3) from the dates the person supplies. When the next annual general meeting must be held is s 175's
-- question (a later wave): it is taken as the date input `the last day of the period within which the next annual general meeting ... is
-- required by law to be held`.
-- A bar answers JUST TRUE where it applies and NOTHING where the text's own words ("without the prior approval") limit it and the case falls
-- outside the limit; a grant named "may" answers NOTHING, not JUST FALSE, when its "if" gate fails (ruling of 2026-11-12, limit 1).

§ `Section 161(1): the bar on issuing shares without the company's prior approval`

DECLARE `s 161(1) act` IS ONE OF
    `s 161(1) exercise a power of the company to issue shares`

-- src:13357 | 161.—(1) Despite anything in a company’s constitution, the
-- src:13358 | directors must not, without the prior approval of the company in
-- src:13359 | general meeting, exercise any power of the company to issue shares.
@ref s 161(1) (src:13357-13359)
GIVEN `the company has given its prior approval in general meeting for the directors to exercise the power` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 161(1) the section bars the directors from exercising the power of the company to issue shares` MEANS
    IF `the company has given its prior approval in general meeting for the directors to exercise the power`
    THEN NOTHING     -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "the directors must not, without the prior approval of the company in general meeting, exercise any power of the company to issue shares" bars the exercise "without" the approval, and grants nothing where approval was given
    ELSE JUST TRUE   -- the text bars it in its own words (a flat negative, ruling N2 of 2026-11-04): "the directors must not, without the prior approval of the company in general meeting, exercise any power of the company to issue shares"

-- The prohibition as a regulative rule, derived from the flat negative (N2): the party is the director who exercises the power.
-- With the approval in hand the rule has nothing left to forbid (FULFILLED); without it, exercising the power is the breach.
-- src:13357 | 161.—(1) Despite anything in a company’s constitution, the
-- src:13358 | directors must not, without the prior approval of the company in
-- src:13359 | general meeting, exercise any power of the company to issue shares.
@ref s 161(1) (src:13357-13359)
GIVEN `the name of the director` IS A STRING
      `the company has given its prior approval in general meeting for the directors to exercise the power` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A DEONTIC Party `s 161(1) act`
`s 161(1) the directors must not exercise the power of the company to issue shares without the company's prior approval` MEANS
    IF `the company has given its prior approval in general meeting for the directors to exercise the power`
    THEN FULFILLED
    ELSE PARTY Director `the name of the director`
         SHANT `s 161(1) exercise a power of the company to issue shares`
         HENCE FULFILLED
         LEST BREACH BY Director `the name of the director` BECAUSE "the directors exercised a power of the company to issue shares without the prior approval of the company in general meeting"

§ `Section 161(2): the forms an approval may take`

DECLARE `s 161(2) scope of approval` IS ONE OF
    `s 161(2) confined to a particular exercise of the power`
    `s 161(2) applying to the exercise of the power generally`
    `s 161(2) some other scope`

DECLARE `s 161(2) conditions of approval` IS ONE OF
    `s 161(2) unconditional`
    `s 161(2) subject to conditions`
    `s 161(2) some other kind of condition`

-- src:13363 | (2) Approval for the purposes of this section may be confined to a
-- src:13364 | particular exercise of that power or may apply to the exercise of that
-- src:13365 | power generally; and any such approval may be unconditional or
-- src:13366 | subject to conditions.
@ref s 161(2) (src:13363-13366)
GIVEN scope IS A `s 161(2) scope of approval`
      conditions IS A `s 161(2) conditions of approval`
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 161(2) the approval is of a form the subsection allows` MEANS
    IF (CONSIDER scope
        WHEN `s 161(2) confined to a particular exercise of the power` THEN TRUE
        WHEN `s 161(2) applying to the exercise of the power generally` THEN TRUE
        OTHERWISE FALSE)   -- FALSE-kind (helper over the list of forms: a form the subsection does not name is not one of its limbs; the enclosing function answers NOTHING for it, see its NOTHING-kind line)
       AND (CONSIDER conditions
            WHEN `s 161(2) unconditional` THEN TRUE
            WHEN `s 161(2) subject to conditions` THEN TRUE
            OTHERWISE FALSE)   -- FALSE-kind (helper over the list of forms: a form the subsection does not name is not one of its limbs; the enclosing function answers NOTHING for it, see its NOTHING-kind line)
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (list not closed): "Approval for the purposes of this section may be confined to a particular exercise of that power or may apply to the exercise of that power generally; and any such approval may be unconditional or subject to conditions" names two scopes and two kinds of condition without saying "only"

-- Whether a given exercise of the power falls within an approval. The subsection says what an approval may be; it does not say what
-- follows for an exercise outside a confined approval or one whose conditions are unmet (fork F-161-2 in NOTES.md).
-- src:13363 | (2) Approval for the purposes of this section may be confined to a
-- src:13364 | particular exercise of that power or may apply to the exercise of that
-- src:13365 | power generally; and any such approval may be unconditional or
-- src:13366 | subject to conditions.
@ref s 161(2) (src:13363-13366)
GIVEN scope IS A `s 161(2) scope of approval`
      `the exercise is the particular exercise the approval is confined to` IS A BOOLEAN
      conditions IS A `s 161(2) conditions of approval`
      `the conditions of the approval are met` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 161(2) the exercise of the power falls within the approval` MEANS
    IF    (CONSIDER scope
           WHEN `s 161(2) confined to a particular exercise of the power` THEN `the exercise is the particular exercise the approval is confined to`
           WHEN `s 161(2) applying to the exercise of the power generally` THEN TRUE
           OTHERWISE FALSE)   -- FALSE-kind (helper over the list of forms: a form the subsection does not name is not one of its limbs; the enclosing function answers NOTHING for it, see its NOTHING-kind line)
      AND (CONSIDER conditions
           WHEN `s 161(2) unconditional` THEN TRUE
           WHEN `s 161(2) subject to conditions` THEN `the conditions of the approval are met`
           OTHERWISE FALSE)   -- FALSE-kind (helper over the list of forms: a form the subsection does not name is not one of its limbs; the enclosing function answers NOTHING for it, see its NOTHING-kind line)
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "may be confined to a particular exercise of that power or may apply to the exercise of that power generally" and "may be unconditional or subject to conditions" describe the forms an approval may take; they do not say what follows for an exercise outside a confined approval or where a condition is unmet

§ `Section 161(3): how long an approval continues in force`

-- The earlier of the two dates the subsection names.
-- (a) the conclusion of the annual general meeting commencing next after the date on which the approval was given;
-- (b) the expiration of the period within which the next annual general meeting after that date is required by law to be held.
-- Reading (fork F-161-3): the approval is taken to continue through the day on which the earlier event falls.
-- src:13368 | (3) Any approval for the purposes of this section continues in force
-- src:13369 | until —
-- src:13371 | (a) the conclusion of the annual general meeting commencing
-- src:13372 | next after the date on which the approval was given; or
-- src:13374 | (b) the expiration of the period within which the next annual
-- src:13375 | general meeting after that date is required by law to be
-- src:13376 | held,
-- src:13378 | whichever is the earlier; but any approval may be previously revoked
-- src:13379 | or varied by the company in general meeting.
@ref s 161(3) (src:13368-13379)
GIVEN `the date the next annual general meeting commencing after the approval was given concludes` IS A DATE
      `the last day of the period within which the next annual general meeting after that date is required by law to be held` IS A DATE
GIVETH A DATE
`s 161(3) the last day on which the approval continues in force` MEANS
    IF `the date the next annual general meeting commencing after the approval was given concludes` AT MOST `the last day of the period within which the next annual general meeting after that date is required by law to be held`
    THEN `the date the next annual general meeting commencing after the approval was given concludes`
    ELSE `the last day of the period within which the next annual general meeting after that date is required by law to be held`

-- src:13368 | (3) Any approval for the purposes of this section continues in force
-- src:13369 | until —
-- src:13371 | (a) the conclusion of the annual general meeting commencing
-- src:13372 | next after the date on which the approval was given; or
-- src:13374 | (b) the expiration of the period within which the next annual
-- src:13375 | general meeting after that date is required by law to be
-- src:13376 | held,
-- src:13378 | whichever is the earlier; but any approval may be previously revoked
-- src:13379 | or varied by the company in general meeting.
@ref s 161(3) (src:13368-13379)
GIVEN `the date the approval was given` IS A DATE
      `the day asked about` IS A DATE
      `the date the next annual general meeting commencing after the approval was given concludes` IS A DATE
      `the last day of the period within which the next annual general meeting after that date is required by law to be held` IS A DATE
      `the company in general meeting revoked the approval before the day asked about` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 161(3) the approval is in force on the day` MEANS
    IF `the company in general meeting revoked the approval before the day asked about`
    THEN JUST FALSE   -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "any approval may be previously revoked or varied by the company in general meeting"
    ELSE IF `the day asked about` LESS THAN `the date the approval was given`
         THEN NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (silent): the subsection says how long an approval "continues in force" and says nothing of a day before it was given
         ELSE IF `the day asked about` AT MOST (`s 161(3) the last day on which the approval continues in force` `the date the next annual general meeting commencing after the approval was given concludes` `the last day of the period within which the next annual general meeting after that date is required by law to be held`)
              THEN JUST TRUE
              ELSE JUST FALSE   -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "Any approval for the purposes of this section continues in force until" the earlier of the two events (a period fixed in the text's own words: the periods ruling of 2026-11-05, item (a))

§ `Section 161(4): shares issued after the approval has ceased`

-- src:13384 | (4) The directors may issue shares even though an approval for the
-- src:13385 | purposes of this section has ceased to be in force if the shares are
-- src:13386 | issued pursuant to an offer, agreement or option made or granted by
-- src:13387 | them while the approval was in force and they were authorised by the
-- src:13388 | approval to make or grant an offer, agreement or option which would
-- src:13389 | or might require shares to be issued after the expiration of the
-- src:13390 | approval.
@ref s 161(4) (src:13384-13390)
GIVEN `the shares are issued pursuant to an offer, agreement or option` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the offer, agreement or option was made or granted by the directors while the approval was in force` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the approval authorised the directors to make or grant an offer, agreement or option which would or might require shares to be issued after the expiration of the approval` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 161(4) the directors may issue the shares although the approval has ceased to be in force` MEANS
    IF    `the shares are issued pursuant to an offer, agreement or option`
      AND `the offer, agreement or option was made or granted by the directors while the approval was in force`
      AND `the approval authorised the directors to make or grant an offer, agreement or option which would or might require shares to be issued after the expiration of the approval`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "The directors may issue shares even though an approval ... has ceased to be in force if the shares are issued pursuant to an offer, agreement or option made or granted by them while the approval was in force" names a grant, and a failed "if" gate on a grant answers NOTHING (the grant rule of 2026-11-12, limit 1; fork F-161-4 in NOTES.md)

§ `Section 161(5): section 186 and the resolution giving the approval`

-- src:13392 | (5) Section 186 applies to any resolution whereby an approval is
-- src:13393 | given for the purposes of this section.
@ref s 161(5) (src:13392-13393)
GIVEN `the resolution gives an approval for the purposes of section 161` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 161(5) section 186 applies to the resolution` MEANS
    IF `the resolution gives an approval for the purposes of section 161`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "Section 186 applies to any resolution whereby an approval is given for the purposes of this section" names this class of resolution; it does not say whether section 186 applies to any other

§ `Section 161(6): an issue made in contravention is void`

-- The two things the subsection says follow from an issue made in contravention. The contravention itself is s 161(1)'s question, combined
-- with the s 161(2), (3) and (4) answers, in the function below.
-- src:13357 | 161.—(1) Despite anything in a company’s constitution, the
-- src:13358 | directors must not, without the prior approval of the company in
-- src:13359 | general meeting, exercise any power of the company to issue shares.
-- src:13384 | (4) The directors may issue shares even though an approval for the
-- src:13385 | purposes of this section has ceased to be in force if the shares are
-- src:13386 | issued pursuant to an offer, agreement or option made or granted by
-- src:13387 | them while the approval was in force and they were authorised by the
-- src:13388 | approval to make or grant an offer, agreement or option which would
-- src:13389 | or might require shares to be issued after the expiration of the
-- src:13390 | approval.
@ref s 161(1), (4) (src:13357-13390)
GIVEN `an approval was given before the directors exercised the power` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the approval was in force when the directors exercised the power` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the exercise falls within the approval` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the shares were issued pursuant to an offer, agreement or option within section 161(4)` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 161 the issue of shares was made in contravention of the section` MEANS
    IF `the shares were issued pursuant to an offer, agreement or option within section 161(4)`
    THEN NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "The directors may issue shares even though an approval ... has ceased to be in force if the shares are issued pursuant to an offer, agreement or option" lifts the bar for these shares and says no more
    ELSE IF    `an approval was given before the directors exercised the power`
           AND `the approval was in force when the directors exercised the power`
           AND `the exercise falls within the approval`
         THEN NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): the bar applies "without the prior approval of the company in general meeting"; with it the section says no more
         ELSE JUST TRUE -- the bar of s 161(1) applies in its own words: "the directors must not, without the prior approval of the company in general meeting, exercise any power of the company to issue shares"

-- src:13395 | (6) Any issue of shares made by a company in contravention of this
-- src:13396 | section is void and consideration given for the shares is recoverable
-- src:13397 | accordingly.
@ref s 161(6) (src:13395-13397)
GIVEN `the issue of shares was made by the company in contravention of section 161` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 161(6) the issue of shares is void` MEANS
    IF `the issue of shares was made by the company in contravention of section 161`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "Any issue of shares made by a company in contravention of this section is void" names the contravening issue and says nothing of any other issue

-- src:13395 | (6) Any issue of shares made by a company in contravention of this
-- src:13396 | section is void and consideration given for the shares is recoverable
-- src:13397 | accordingly.
@ref s 161(6) (src:13395-13397)
GIVEN `the issue of shares was made by the company in contravention of section 161` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 161(6) the consideration given for the shares is recoverable` MEANS
    IF `the issue of shares was made by the company in contravention of section 161`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "consideration given for the shares is recoverable accordingly" follows only the contravening issue; the text does not say by whom or from whom it is recoverable (fork F-161-6 in NOTES.md)

§ `Section 161(7): a director's liability to compensate, and the two years`

-- src:13399 | (7) Any director who knowingly contravenes, or permits or
-- src:13400 | authorises the contravention of, this section with respect to any
-- src:13401 | issue of shares shall be liable to compensate the company and the
-- src:13402 | person to whom the shares were issued for any loss, damages or costs
-- src:13403 | which the company or that person may have sustained or incurred
-- src:13404 | thereby; but no proceedings to recover any such loss, damages or
-- src:13405 | costs may be commenced after the expiration of 2 years from the date
-- src:13406 | of the issue.
@ref s 161(7) (src:13399-13406)
GIVEN `the director knowingly contravened section 161 with respect to the issue of shares` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the director permitted the contravention of section 161 with respect to the issue of shares` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the director authorised the contravention of section 161 with respect to the issue of shares` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 161(7) the director is liable to compensate the company and the person to whom the shares were issued` MEANS
    IF    `the director knowingly contravened section 161 with respect to the issue of shares`
       OR `the director permitted the contravention of section 161 with respect to the issue of shares`
       OR `the director authorised the contravention of section 161 with respect to the issue of shares`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "Any director who knowingly contravenes, or permits or authorises the contravention of, this section" describes the directors it reaches (a scope limit with no "only": the periods ruling of 2026-11-05, item (c)); it says nothing of another director

-- "no proceedings to recover any such loss, damages or costs may be commenced after the expiration of 2 years from the date of the issue".
-- Written as the bar: JUST TRUE where the proceedings are out of time; NOTHING where they are within the 2 years (the subsection grants no right to sue).
-- src:13399 | (7) Any director who knowingly contravenes, or permits or
-- src:13400 | authorises the contravention of, this section with respect to any
-- src:13401 | issue of shares shall be liable to compensate the company and the
-- src:13402 | person to whom the shares were issued for any loss, damages or costs
-- src:13403 | which the company or that person may have sustained or incurred
-- src:13404 | thereby; but no proceedings to recover any such loss, damages or
-- src:13405 | costs may be commenced after the expiration of 2 years from the date
-- src:13406 | of the issue.
@ref s 161(7) (src:13399-13406)
GIVEN `the date of the issue of the shares` IS A DATE
      `the date the proceedings are commenced` IS A DATE
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 161(7) the 2 years bar the proceedings to recover the loss, damages or costs` MEANS
    IF `period: the date is not later than the end of a period after` `the date of the issue of the shares` `the date the proceedings are commenced` 2 Years
    THEN NOTHING     -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "no proceedings ... may be commenced after the expiration of 2 years from the date of the issue" bars late proceedings and says nothing of proceedings within the 2 years
    ELSE JUST TRUE   -- the text bars it in its own words (a flat negative inside a period the text fixes: the periods ruling of 2026-11-05, item (a)): "no proceedings to recover any such loss, damages or costs may be commenced after the expiration of 2 years from the date of the issue"
